---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PianoKeyboard from '../../components/PianoKeyboard.astro';
import {releaseChords} from '../../data/chords.js';
import {createChord,prettyNote} from '../../lib/music.js';
const major=createChord(releaseChords.find(c=>c.slug==='c-major'));
const minor=createChord(releaseChords.find(c=>c.slug==='b-minor'));
---
<BaseLayout title="How to Read Piano Chord Diagrams | Beginner's Guide" description="Understand piano chord diagrams, root notes, thirds, fifths, accidentals, major and minor chords, and inversions with original visual examples." canonicalPath="/guides/how-to-read-piano-chords/" crumbs={[{name:'Reading Piano Chords',href:'/guides/how-to-read-piano-chords/'}]}>
<div class="container page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Piano chord basics</span><h1>How to read<br/>piano chord diagrams.</h1><p class="lead">A beginner-friendly guide to the keys, note spellings, chord shapes and inversions you will see throughout this website.</p></div>
<div class="container section-compact two-col"><article class="prose"><h2>1. Start with the note names</h2><p>Piano repeats the same twelve pitch classes in every octave. White keys are the natural notes C, D, E, F, G, A and B. Black keys are generally named using sharps (♯) or flats (♭), depending on the musical context.</p><p>In a chord diagram, highlighted keys show which notes to play. Read the note list as well as the picture: an F♯ and a G♭ use the same piano key in equal temperament, but they play different theoretical roles in different chords.</p><h2>2. Identify the root, third and fifth</h2><p>For <a class="text-link" href="/chords/c-major/">C major</a>, the three notes are C, E and G. C is the root. E is the major third, four semitones above C. G is a perfect fifth, seven semitones above C.</p><PianoKeyboard chord={major}/><div class="button-row no-print"><button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-play-midi={major.inversions[0].midi.join(',')}>▶ Hear C major</button></div><h2>3. Hear the difference in a minor chord</h2><p>Minor triads lower the third by one semitone. <a class="text-link" href="/chords/b-minor/">B minor</a> is spelled B–D–F♯. Notice how its D natural changes the feel of the chord compared with B major's D♯.</p><PianoKeyboard chord={minor}/><div class="button-row no-print"><button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-play-midi={minor.inversions[0].midi.join(',')}>▶ Hear B minor</button></div><h2>4. Inversions change the lowest note</h2><p>A chord can keep its identity while the lowest note changes. In C major, root position is C–E–G; first inversion is E–G–C; second inversion is G–C–E. These are voicings of the same C major harmony, not three unrelated chords.</p><h2>5. What does a seventh mean?</h2><p>A seventh chord adds another note. C7 is C–E–G–B♭, while Cmaj7 is C–E–G–B. They are distinct chords and should not be treated as aliases. Explore <a class="text-link" href="/chords/c-7/">C7</a>, <a class="text-link" href="/chords/c-major-7/">Cmaj7</a>, <a class="text-link" href="/chords/a-minor-7/">Am7</a> and the <a class="text-link" href="/guides/seventh-chords-piano/">seventh-chord comparison guide</a>. The numbers and accidentals distinguish genuinely different chords.</p><h2>6. Practice with a small routine</h2><p>Choose one chord, say its note names, play it together, then play each note separately. Switch to an inversion and listen to the lower voice. Repeat with another chord before attempting a progression.</p><p>Finger numbers are suggestions, not universal rules. A comfortable fingering depends on hand size, register, tempo and musical setting.</p></article><aside><div class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">Next steps</span><h3>Practice with the tools</h3><p>Turn the concepts into sound and muscle memory.</p><div style="display:grid;gap:10px"><a class="btn" href="/chords/">Explore the chord library</a><a class="btn" href="/tools/chord-finder/">Use the chord finder</a><a class="btn" href="/tools/inversion-explorer/">Compare inversions</a><a class="btn" href="/printables/piano-chord-chart/">Print a reference sheet</a></div></div></aside></div>
</BaseLayout>
